@@ -196,7 +196,7 @@ A raft is delivered as a stack: `./zn start` opens one layer per ticket (a branc
 ## The five parts
 - **The tracker.** `./zn` at the repository root runs the engine in `z-nile/engine/`. Each project keeps its issues and rafts in its own root, `<project>/.tracker/` (z-nile's in `z-nile/.tracker/`), listed in `z-nile/projects.toml`. One counter, `z-nile/next`, numbers every project. The rules are in the zz-rules skill.
 - **The skills.** z-nile's skills live in `z-nile/skills/`, and a project's own in `<project>/skills/`. `.claude/skills/<name>` is a committed relative link to each, written by `./zn skills` and checked by `./zn skills --check` in the gate. The `z-` skills are the pipeline, in the order above; the `zz-` skills are called only from inside a step. Both prefixes are z-nile's: a project's skills are named `<project>-…`.
-- **The dispatcher.** `./zn dispatch <id>` runs the chain on an item from its tracker state (ticket, design, raft, ready, run), headless and unattended, up to the run's pull request: script steps (`ready`, `raft-plan`, `spend`, …) it runs itself, model steps on the CLIs `.models.toml` orders, moving down the order only when a CLI fails on quota. It stops at a merge gate, at a step's `questions.md` and at a failure, and records each step's tokens and model on its spend row. A running step appends one short line per action (the tool and its target, or the first words of a message) to `.cache/dispatch/<id>/trail.log`, and `./zn dispatch status <id>` prints the last of those lines and the running token count before the step's JSON. Agents run with `z-nile/bin/git` and `gh` first on their PATH. Agents run as your own OS user, so its guards (the wrapper, the publish broker, the tripwire, the merge gates) detect honest mistakes and casual misbehaviour; none of them is a security boundary against a determined agent. A run accepts `main` as it is when it starts, and during the run a rewritten `main` or a commit that is not tracker state and not a pull request's merge stops it. The rules are in the zz-rules skill, Dispatch.
+- **The dispatcher.** `./zn dispatch <id>` runs the chain on an item from its tracker state (ticket, design, raft, ready, run), headless and unattended, up to the run's pull request: script steps (`ready`, `raft-plan`, `spend`, …) it runs itself, model steps on the CLIs `.models.toml` orders, moving down the order only when a CLI fails on quota. It stops at a merge gate, at a step's `questions.md` and at a failure, and records each step's tokens and model on its spend row. Its `design` step drafts a design that a person approves: the chain merges the prep and rafts it without waiting, but it stops (exit 3, waiting for the person, not a failure) before it readies a raft any of whose issues has no approval on `origin/main` that matches its contract. The stop names each issue and the commands that approve it in a terminal of your own (`./zn approve <id>`, then `./zn approve <id> --publish`), and then `./zn dispatch <R-id>` goes on to `z-ready`. A running step appends one short line per action (the tool and its target, or the first words of a message) to `.cache/dispatch/<id>/trail.log`, and `./zn dispatch status <id>` prints the last of those lines and the running token count before the step's JSON. Agents run with `z-nile/bin/git` and `gh` first on their PATH. Agents run as your own OS user, so its guards (the wrapper, the publish broker, the tripwire, the merge gates) detect honest mistakes and casual misbehaviour; none of them is a security boundary against a determined agent. A run accepts `main` as it is when it starts, and during the run a rewritten `main` or a commit that is not tracker state and not a pull request's merge stops it. The rules are in the zz-rules skill, Dispatch.
 - **`.models.toml`.** It says, per model step and CLI (`claude`, `grok`, `agent`), the model, its rank, runtime and `max_tokens`, and the CLI priority order, stacking from the repository root down to the work, as `.gitignore` does.
 - **The board.** `./zn board` writes the whole tracker as one page (`.cache/board.html`): a tab per project, its issues as cards in the pipeline's columns with each raft a band behind its members, the review charts, and the next command for each row (c0118). `./zn board --serve` (c0121, planned) serves the same page live.
 
diff --git a/z-nile/engine/approval.luau b/z-nile/engine/approval.luau
index dc2720272..2d8948a76 100644
--- a/z-nile/engine/approval.luau
+++ b/z-nile/engine/approval.luau
@@ -107,6 +107,24 @@ function approval.verdict(root: string, item: store.Item): (Verdict?, string?)
 	return { state = "changed", changed = changed, row = row, err = nil }, nil
 end
 
+-- Whether approval.md's latest row matches the contract: "none" (no file), "bad" (it does not parse, with why),
+-- "holds", or "changed" (with the parts that changed). The one reading of an approval's text: reasonOf and
+-- readycheck.waiting both go through it.
+function approval.stateOf(parts: contract.Parts, approvalText: string?): (string, { string }, string?)
+	if not approvalText then
+		return "none", {}, nil
+	end
+	local rows, perr = contract.parse(approvalText)
+	if not rows then
+		return "bad", {}, perr
+	end
+	local changed = contract.changed(rows[#rows], parts)
+	if #changed == 0 then
+		return "holds", {}, nil
+	end
+	return "changed", changed, nil
+end
+
 -- The refusal from texts `ready` and `work next` both already have. `required` is ready's rule (a missing
 -- approval refuses). done and land leave an issue with no approval.md alone.
 function approval.reasonOf(
@@ -116,18 +134,17 @@ function approval.reasonOf(
 	approvalText: string?,
 	required: boolean
 ): string?
-	if not approvalText then
+	local state, changed, perr = approval.stateOf(parts, approvalText)
+	if state == "none" then
 		if not required then
 			return nil
 		end
 		return `{id} has no approval: run ./zn approve {id}, then ./zn approve {id} --publish, from the worktree that holds the design. This is a one-time step for an item prepared before approval.`
 	end
-	local rows, perr = contract.parse(approvalText)
-	if not rows then
+	if state == "bad" then
 		return `{id}: {fileRel} does not parse: {perr}`
 	end
-	local changed = contract.changed(rows[#rows], parts)
-	if #changed == 0 then
+	if state == "holds" then
 		return nil
 	end
 	return `{id}'s contract changed since approval: {table.concat(changed, ", ")}. Run ./zn approve {id} again, then ./zn approve {id} --publish.`
