+-- The issues of backlog raft `id` that wait for a person's approval on origin/main (readycheck.waiting, in the
+-- raft's layer order), read the way `./zn work next` reads it. Nil and why when it cannot be read; an empty list
+-- when ready would refuse the raft for something else first, or would accept it.
+function workread.unapproved(id: string): ({ readycheck.Unapproved }?, string?)
+	local ok, input = pcall(workread.collect, id)
+	if not ok then
+		return nil, tostring(input)
+	end
+	for _, r in (input :: work.Input).rafts do
+		if r.id == id then
+			return r.unapproved or {}, nil
+		end
+	end
+	return nil, `{id} is not a raft on origin/main`
+end
+
 return workread
diff --git a/z-nile/skills/z-design/SKILL.md b/z-nile/skills/z-design/SKILL.md
index 668b34d80..d308702df 100644
--- a/z-nile/skills/z-design/SKILL.md
+++ b/z-nile/skills/z-design/SKILL.md
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@ One clean reviewer, one round. The designer answers every finding once, fixed or
    - **Harvest the round** as you answer it: add one entry per finding of `design-review-NNN.md` to `harvest.md` in the issue's folder, naming a new lesson, a new source for an existing one (prefer this), or `one-off: <reason>` (format: zz-rules skill, Items). A finding that repeats a lesson sharpens that lesson's `check`. Then run `./zn lessons --check`, and rerun `./zn promote-check <id>` after the round's spec edits (the test runner does not type-check). The lessons and the ledger ride in the prep commit.
 4. **Then the human.** After that one revision, anything you refuted, or any high finding you could not close (not answered `fixed`), goes in the issue document under `## Open to the human`, one bullet each: `- F2: <the finding>. Answer: <your answer>. Decision: <what the human must decide>`. The design is ready when that list is empty or every bullet carries `Ruled (user, YYYY-MM-DD): <their decision>`. Do not start another reviewer, and do not guess a ruling. Moving the issue to prepared, and `ready`, both refuse while a bullet that needs a ruling has none.
 5. **Confirm the harvest**: first harvest anything `./zn lessons --uncovered <id>` still lists (reviews written before lessons existed, or a review that slipped), as step 3 says. Then `./zn lessons --uncovered <id>` and `./zn lessons --check` must both pass. Then **check the scope**: compare the issue's document with `./zn capture <id>` (**Scope is what was captured**, above). Its title, Problem and Solution ideas may only have gained detail and decision lines, and every captured outcome has a Design and an acceptance criterion. Anything else goes to the user before `design: ready`.
-   When the open list is empty or ruled, set `design: ready` on the issue, **then run `./zn status <id> prepared`** (carried: it edits the local document, and refuses unless the issue is `design: ready` and `preparing` on `origin/main`, and unless the open list still has an unruled bullet), and only then make the prep commit (`status` needs an origin: with `PICKMAN_PUBLISH=0` or no origin, edit the `status:` line by hand to `prepared-<three words>` instead). Without it the issue keeps its `preparing` stamp and z-raft can't group it. Leave `raft:` empty: z-raft writes it. Stop and ask the human to run `./zn approve <id>` in a terminal in this worktree. Do not run it yourself.
+   When the open list is empty or ruled, set `design: ready` on the issue, **then run `./zn status <id> prepared`** (carried: it edits the local document, and refuses unless the issue is `design: ready` and `preparing` on `origin/main`, and unless the open list still has an unruled bullet), and only then make the prep commit (`status` needs an origin: with `PICKMAN_PUBLISH=0` or no origin, edit the `status:` line by hand to `prepared-<three words>` instead). Without it the issue keeps its `preparing` stamp and z-raft can't group it. Leave `raft:` empty: z-raft writes it. Stop and ask the human to run `./zn approve <id>` in a terminal in this worktree. Do not run it yourself. Under the dispatcher you do not ask: the chain merges the prep and rafts the issue, and stops before the raft is readied until a person has approved it.
 
 ## 6. Commit and hand off
 - **Commit** the prepared issue, its acceptance specs and architecture changes in the prep worktree as one commit, `tracker: prep c0010 <title>`, then follow Landing a change (zz-rules skill): push, PR, merge, pull the main checkout, remove the worktree. Right after the prep PR is opened, before it merges (so before any raft holding it can be readied), `./zn spend <id> --phase prep` (once: it counts the prep branch's lines). An issue readied first can no longer take it, and `spend` says so.
